==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*per-|id1=before|*weyd-}}
{{inh+|en|enm|providen}}, from {{der|en|la|prōvideō|prōvidēre|t=to foresee, act with foresight}}. {{dbt|en|purvey}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|prə-vīdʹ}}, {{IPA|en|/pɹəˈvaɪd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-provide.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪd|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|pro|vide}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To make a living; earn money for [[necessities]].
#: {{ux|en|It is difficult to '''provide''' for my family working on minimum wage.}}
# To [[act]] to [[prepare]] for something.
#: {{ux|en|provide '''against''' disaster.}}
# To establish as a previous condition; to [[stipulate]].
#: {{ux|en|The contract '''provides''' that the work be well done.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'll lend you the money, '''provided''' that you pay it back by Monday.}}
# To give what is needed or desired, especially basic [[need]]s.
#: {{ux|en|Don't bother bringing equipment, as we will '''provide''' it.}}
#: {{ux|en|We aim to '''provide''' the local community with more green spaces.}}
#: {{ux|en|This resort '''provide'''s an evening meal for its guests.}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Jeanne Sahadi|title=Lawyers fear Trump may kill legal aid funding for the poor|url=https://money.cnn.com/2017/03/09/news/economy/trump-budget-cuts/|date=2017-03-09|passage=LSC '''provides''' grants to nonprofits nationwide that in turn provide civil legal aid to those who otherwise can't afford it. The beneficiaries of LSC funding include homeless veterans, low-income workers and victims of domestic abuse.|accessdate=5 May 2025|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250505075309/https://money.cnn.com/2017/03/09/news/economy/trump-budget-cuts/|archivedate=5 May 2025}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|title=UN says climate change ‘out of control’ after likely hottest week on record|date=2023-07-07|url=https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/jul/07/un-climate-change-hottest-week-world|passage=Climate Reanalyzer uses data from the NCEP climate forecast system to '''provide''' a time series of daily mean two-metre air temperature, based on readings from surface, air balloon and satellite observations.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261017220843/https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/jul/07/un-climate-change-hottest-week-world|archivedate=17 October 2026}}
# To [[furnish]] (with), cause to be present, supply.
#: {{syn|en|afford|treat}}
#* {{RQ:Arbuthnot Coins
|passage=Rome {{...}} was generally well '''provided''' with corn.}}
# To [[make]] possible or attainable.
#: {{ux|en|He '''provides''' us with an alternative option.}}
#: {{ux|en|Plants '''provide''' (us) with the oxygen we breathe.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Comus|passage=Bring me berries, or such cooling fruit / As the kind, hospitable woods '''provide'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|[[Latinism]]}} To [[foresee]], to [[consider]] [[in advance]].
#* {{RQ:Jonson Sejanus|passage=We have not been covetous, honourable fathers, to change, neither is it now any new lust that alters our affection, or old lothing, but those needful jealousies of state, that warn wiser princes hourly to '''provide''' their safety, and do teach them how learned a thing it is to beware of the humblest enemy; much more of those great ones, whom their own employed favours have made fit for their fears.}}
# To appoint to an [[ecclesiastical]] [[benefice]] before it is [[vacant]]. See ''[[provisor]]''.
#* {{RQ:Prescott Ferdinand and Isabella|passage='''provide''' such natives to the higher dignities of the church}}

====Usage notes====
* As seen in the examples, when not used with ''that'' for previous conditions, ''provide'' is used with the prepositions ''for'' (beneficiary; also without preposition, usual for pronouns) and ''with'' (object).

